## Method and reasoning
Euclid replaces (a,b) by (b,a mod b) without changing the GCD. Divide before multiplying for the LCM. Extended Euclid returns coefficients x,y with ax+by=gcd(a,b); if the GCD is one, normalize x modulo b to get the unique inverse. Reduce signed inputs before recursing.

## Walk through the sample
For INV −3 11, normalize −3 to 8. Since 8 × 7 = 56 leaves remainder 1 modulo 11, the answer is 7. For 4 modulo 10 the gcd is 2, so no product can leave remainder 1.

## Complexity
O(log(max(a,b)+1)) arithmetic steps per query, with O(log(max(a,b)+1)) recursion depth.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

pair<long long, long long> coefficients(long long a, long long b) {
    if (b == 0)
        return {1, 0};
    auto [x, y] = coefficients(b, a % b);
    return {y, x - a / b * y};
}
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int q;
    cin >> q;
    while (q--) {
        string op;
        long long a, b;
        cin >> op >> a >> b;
        long long g = gcd(a, b);
        if (op == "GCD")
            cout << g << '\n';
        else if (op == "LCM")
            cout << (g ? a / g * b : 0) << '\n';
        else if (g != 1)
            cout << "NONE\n";
        else {
            auto [x, y] = coefficients((a % b + b) % b, b);
            cout << (x % b + b) % b << '\n';
        }
    }
}
~~~
